# Propagating equivalent property statements
#
# Three names for the same relationship (parent, hasChild, child) are
# declared equivalent in a chain. Plain N3 rules spell out what OWL's
# owl:equivalentProperty means: equivalence is symmetric and transitive, it
# amounts to two-way rdfs:subPropertyOf, and a statement made with one
# property also holds with every equivalent one. Eyeron derives that Alice
# is related to Bob under all three names.

@prefix : <http://example.org/> .
@prefix owl: <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix list: <http://www.w3.org/2000/10/swap/list#> .
@prefix log: <http://www.w3.org/2000/10/swap/log#> .

# Two equivalence declarations and one statement using the first name
:parent owl:equivalentProperty :hasChild .
:hasChild owl:equivalentProperty :child .
:alice :parent :bob .

# Rules giving owl:equivalentProperty its meaning
@prefix owl: <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .

# A statement with P also holds with an equivalent Q ...
{
    ?P owl:equivalentProperty ?Q .
    ?S ?P ?O
} => {
    ?S ?Q ?O
} .

# ... and a statement with Q also holds with P.
{
    ?P owl:equivalentProperty ?Q .
    ?S ?Q ?O
} => {
    ?S ?P ?O
} .

# Equivalence is symmetric.
{
    ?P owl:equivalentProperty ?Q
} => {
    ?Q owl:equivalentProperty ?P
} .

# Equivalence is transitive; with symmetry this also makes each property
# equivalent to itself.
{
    ?P owl:equivalentProperty ?Q .
    ?Q owl:equivalentProperty ?R
} => {
    ?P owl:equivalentProperty ?R
} .

# Equivalent properties are subproperties of each other ...
{
    ?P owl:equivalentProperty ?Q
} => {
    ?P rdfs:subPropertyOf ?Q .
    ?Q rdfs:subPropertyOf ?P
} .

# ... and mutual subproperties are equivalent.
{
    ?P rdfs:subPropertyOf ?Q .
    ?Q rdfs:subPropertyOf ?P
} => {
    ?P owl:equivalentProperty ?Q
} .
